22:52:30 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: While it might seem like supersonic flight was “abolished,” it wasn’t banned by a single law—it became commercially unviable, and the Concorde’s retirement in 2003 was the culmination of several intertwined problems. Here’s a breakdown of why the Concorde stopped flying and why no replacement has succeeded so far. ### 1. Crippling Operating Costs - **Fuel hunger**: The Concorde burned about 25,000 liters of fuel per hour—roughly **four times** the fuel per passenger of a subsonic jumbo jet. After the 1970s oil crises and rising jet fuel prices, profitability became nearly impossible. - **Tiny passenger capacity**: With only 100–128 seats, ticket prices had to be astronomical to break even (a round-trip transatlantic ticket often cost more than $10,000 in today's money). This limited the market to a tiny elite. - **Maintenance nightmares**: The aircraft was an engineering marvel from the 1960s, but by the early 2000s its aging systems and unique spare parts were increasingly scarce and expensive to maintain. ### 2. The Sonic Boom Ban Over Land - Concorde could only fly supersonic over water because its sonic boom was extremely loud and disruptive. This banned it from lucrative overland routes (e.g., cross-USA, Europe-Asia overland), confining it almost exclusively to transatlantic flights between London/Paris and New York/Washington. - This route restriction choked any chance of broad network expansion. ### 3. Environmental and Noise Opposition - Besides the boom, Concorde was deafeningly loud on takeoff and landing, far noisier than contemporary jets. This led to airport restrictions and community opposition. - Although not the primary reason for its withdrawal, growing awareness of the climate impact of high-altitude emissions (nitrogen oxides, water vapor) added to the negative public perception. ### 4. The Fatal Crash of 2000 - The Air France Flight 4590 crash in July 2000 killed all 109 people on board and 4 on the ground. Although the accident was caused by debris on the runway, the tragedy shattered Concorde’s stellar safety image and drastically reduced passenger demand. - When flights resumed after a 16-month grounding and multimillion-dollar safety modifications, demand never fully recovered. ### 5. The Post-9/11 Air Travel Collapse - The September 11, 2001 attacks caused a global slump in premium air travel. Business class travelers—Concorde’s core clientele—cut back sharply, and airlines faced financial crises. Concorde’s slim margins vanished. **Does this mean supersonic flight is “abolished” forever?** Not exactly. It wasn’t banned; it simply failed commercially. Several companies (like Boom Supersonic) are now developing new supersonic airliners with more efficient engines, lower sonic booms (“quiet boom” technology), and the promise of sustainable aviation fuels. Whether they can crack the economic and environmental challenges remains to be seen, but the *concept* of supersonic passenger flight is very much alive—just waiting for a viable business model.
